2. PALLU print: one pleated panel that comes from behind over the left shoulder and hangs straight down the front of her left side, from the shoulder to just above the hem. This panel, and only this panel, is cream PALLU print. Its full length is visible from top to bottom in the same order as the reference photo: the blue vine band section nearest the shoulder, the peacock across the middle, and the scalloped bell-motif edge at the very bottom.
3. BODY print: everything else on the saree: the waist, the pleats at the front of the waist, the whole skirt to the hem, all fabric to the right of the pallu panel, and the band of fabric that crosses the chest from the right hip up to the left shoulder. That chest band is deep blue BODY print with the orange border along its upper edge; it is never cream. The band is wide, not a strip: the deep blue fabric lies on her LEFT side of the border line and covers her left breast, her left ribs and her waist completely, so the blouse is visible only on her right shoulder and the right side of her chest. Deep blue BODY print has no peacock, no pallu motif and no cream pallu ground on it.
4. BORDER: along the hem, along the upper edge of the chest band, and along both long edges of the hanging pallu panel, exactly as narrow as shown.
Transition rule: the cream PALLU print is confined to the single hanging panel on her left side. Its two long edges are the orange border; where the border ends, the deep blue BODY print begins.
