2. PALLU print: one pleated panel that comes from behind over the left shoulder and hangs straight down the front of her left side, from the shoulder to just above the hem. This panel, and only this panel, is cream PALLU print. Its full length is visible from top to bottom in the same order as the reference photo: the blue vine band section nearest the shoulder, the peacock across the middle, and the scalloped bell-motif edge at the very bottom.
3. BODY print: everything else on the saree: the waist, the pleats at the front of the waist, the whole skirt to the hem, all fabric to the right of the pallu panel, and the band of fabric that crosses the chest from the right hip up to the left shoulder. That chest band is deep blue BODY print with the orange border along its upper edge; it is never cream. The band is wide, not a strip: the deep blue fabric lies on her LEFT side of the border line and covers her left breast, her left ribs and her waist completely, so the blouse is visible only on her right shoulder and the right side of her chest. Deep blue BODY print has no peacock, no pallu motif and no cream pallu ground on it.
4. BORDER: along the hem, along the upper edge of the chest band, and along both long edges of the hanging pallu panel, exactly as narrow as shown.
Transition rule: the cream PALLU print is confined to the single hanging panel on her left side. Its two long edges are the orange border; where the border ends, the deep blue BODY print begins.
Not allowed: no pallu motif on the skirt. No cream fabric on the chest band. No blouse fabric and no bare skin visible on her left side above the waist; the chest band covers it. No zari or border trim on the blouse. The pallu panel must not be folded back, bunched, or hidden behind the arm. The pallu must not reach the floor or pool on the ground; it ends just above the hem.

POSE: A professional fashion catalog photo of a woman in her mid-20s wearing this saree. She stands in a relaxed, natural three-quarter pose, her weight shifted onto one leg and her hip gently angled toward the camera, the way a person naturally stands when resting on one side, with her left side nearer the camera so the hanging pallu panel faces the camera. Her right hand rests lightly on her right hip; her left arm hangs naturally at her side beside the pallu panel, not covering it. Her head and neck follow the natural line of her shoulders, with a soft, easy turn of the face toward the camera, no strain or awkward angle between head and body. The pallu is gathered into 5 to 7 narrow, straight, parallel pleats at the left shoulder and falls forward down the front of her body, flat and open, so its full length, pattern and border are visible to just above the hem. The pleats are clean, straight and evenly spaced, like pressed fabric folds, not bunched or crumpled. The blouse has short sleeves ending above the elbow, forearms bare. She has a direct, confident gaze and a neutral-to-soft expression. Full-length portrait, tightly framed so her figure fills most of the vertical frame, with the top of her head, the hem of the saree and the ground under her feet all inside the frame, shot at eye level with minimal headroom.

SCENE: A sunlit traditional Indian courtyard, framed by a stone arch behind her, with pillars on either side, background softly blurred. Hair tied back in a neat low bun. Styled with gold jhumka earrings, a gold choker-style necklace, and bangles. Warm, golden directional lighting.

OUTPUT: Return only the image: ONE photograph in portrait orientation, 3:4, taller than wide, with the one model filling the frame. Not a pair of views, not a grid, not a collage. No caption, no notes, no commentary."
